drm: h713: place a smaller video picture on the panel (letterbox)

The video plane took exactly one geometry, a 1280x720 picture at the origin,
so every source that is not 16:9 -- or is smaller than the panel -- went to
the GPU. This lets the plane show any NV12 picture from 64x64 up to the panel
size, unscaled, anywhere on the panel, with black bars around it. Stock
kmssink then letterboxes by itself: it negotiates the stream's own size,
tries an aspect-fit destination and, on the refusal to scale, falls back to
the picture centred at native size; for a picture the VE has already scaled
to its fit size the two are the same.

With the MIPS parked, nothing places the picture but this driver. The model
is the display firmware's own window layer, recovered from display.bin and
checked on hardware (docs/reference/letterbox-recipe-2026-10-04.md and
letterbox-phase1-2026-10-04.md); tools/display/letterbox-recipe.py is the
single source of the formulas and this patch's table transcribes it -- all
105 register words for the three Phase 1 geometries compared equal on the
host.

  - Placement is an offset added to the NR (composition) output origin.
    Proc copies its input position to its output; the panel's program window
    stays put; the bars are the vblender border overlay at 0x05200000. NR's
    fetch window (0x05000804-0x0500085c) carries the picture size -- left at
    1280 it is what sheared every small source on 2026-09-12.
  - comp+0x858[31:16] is the picture height. Left at the panel's 720 it
    clipped the bottom of every vertically offset picture; three handheld
    bisect runs found it (2026-10-05).
  - The window layer is written in the firmware's order, inside its NR
    hold/release bracket (NRWinNode::WriteReg flags 0x100c0 / 0x8120), with
    video source 0 stopped. The panel shows green for the ~30 ms that takes,
    so a geometry change flashes once; same-geometry flips are unchanged.
  - AFBD's own geometry now follows the picture: whole 16x16 blocks, the
    even width, 4-aligned line lengths, a fetch budget of H lines (2H for
    P010). For 1280x720 these are exactly the constants it always wrote.

atomic_check refuses everything the hardware cannot do: a stride that is
not a multiple of 16 samples (AFBD rounds it up and would shear), a buffer
that does not cover the block-aligned fetch (it would read past it and fault
the IOMMU), odd sizes or x, anything off the panel, scaling, and P010 at
anything but native size (the line-buffer descriptors are not derived for
it). Native-size commits keep exactly the old checks.

The window layer's registers need three more ranges (comp, proc, vblend).
A device tree without them, or a board whose registers do not read back as
the model predicts for 1280x720 -- checked field by field at probe, as the
tool's self-test does -- keeps the native-only plane and the old minimum
framebuffer size. Disabling the plane puts every register back exactly as
probe found it.

The minimum framebuffer size comes down to 64x64 in the same change, as
0063 warned it must: a minimum the plane cannot honour only moves the failure
from caps negotiation to commit.

